  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 4,
    "lesson_title": "Meaning of Fourier Coefficients",
    "lesson_core_move": "Interpret $a_0$, $a_k$, and $b_k$ as the DC level and signed cosine and sine harmonic weights that describe the frequency-domain content of a periodic continuous-time signal.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Meaning of Fourier Coefficients",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Meaning of Fourier Coefficients",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A trigonometric Fourier series is a weighted sum of one DC term and infinitely many sinusoidal harmonics. Read the template as a coefficient map: the subscripts tell you which harmonic you are looking at, and the coefficient names tell you whether the term is a constant, a cosine weight, or a sine weight.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=\\frac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{k=1}^{\\infty}\\big[a_k\\cos(k\\omega_0 t)+b_k\\sin(k\\omega_0 t)\\big]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The term $\\frac{a_0}{2}$ is the DC level. It is the average value or vertical offset of the signal, so it appears once outside the harmonic sum rather than repeating at every frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{DC level}=\\frac{a_0}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For each positive integer $k$, the pair $a_k\\cos(k\\omega_0 t)$ and $b_k\\sin(k\\omega_0 t)$ is the $k$th harmonic. The signs of $a_k$ and $b_k$ matter: they tell you whether that cosine or sine contribution is positive, negative, or absent.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\text{harmonic }k\\text{ lives at }k\\omega_0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the simplest base case: $\\frac{a_0}{2}$ plus the first harmonic pair $a_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+b_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)$. That base case makes the coefficient roles visible before the next examples interpret one coefficient type at a time and then combine them.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Meaning of Fourier Coefficients",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Interpreting the DC Level from $a_0$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A periodic signal is written in trigonometric Fourier series form with $a_0=6$. Explain what the DC level is and how it affects the whole signal.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the constant term $\\frac{a_0}{2}$ in the Fourier series. For $a_0=6$, the DC level is the constant offset obtained by halving $a_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{a_0}{2}=\\frac{6}{2}=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signal is shifted upward by $3$ at every time instant. That constant term does not oscillate; it is the average value of the signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Meaning of Fourier Coefficients",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Signed Cosine Weights from $a_k$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A signal has $a_1=3$ and $a_2=-2$ in its trigonometric Fourier series. Explain what the positive and negative cosine coefficients say about the harmonics at $\\omega_0$ and $2\\omega_0$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the cosine coefficient $a_k$. After reading $\\frac{a_0}{2}$ as the DC level, read each $a_k$ as the signed weight multiplying the cosine harmonic at frequency $k\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "a_1\\cos(\\omega_0 t)+a_2\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)=3\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-2\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $a_1=3$ means a positive cosine contribution at $\\omega_0$, while $a_2=-2$ means the $2\\omega_0$ cosine is inverted and scaled by $2$. The magnitude tells the strength of the harmonic, and the sign tells its direction.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If $a_k=0$, that cosine harmonic is absent. Read $a_k$ as the weight on the cosine basis at harmonic $k$, not as the frequency itself.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Meaning of Fourier Coefficients",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Signed Sine Weights from $b_k$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A signal has $b_1=-4$ and $b_3=2$ in its trigonometric Fourier series. Explain what the signed sine coefficients say about the harmonics at $\\omega_0$ and $3\\omega_0$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the sine coefficient $b_k$. Read each $b_k$ as the signed weight multiplying the sine harmonic at frequency $k\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "b_1\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+b_3\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)=-4\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+2\\sin(3\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $b_1=-4$ means an inverted sine contribution at $\\omega_0$, while $b_3=2$ means a positive sine contribution at $3\\omega_0$. The magnitude tells the strength of the harmonic, and the sign tells its direction.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If $b_k=0$, that sine harmonic is absent. Read $b_k$ as the weight on the sine basis at harmonic $k$, not as the frequency itself.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 4,
        "lesson_title": "Meaning of Fourier Coefficients",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Identifying Present and Missing Harmonics from Coefficients",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A coefficient table gives $a_0=4$, $a_1=0$, $a_2=-3$, $b_1=5$, and $b_2=0$. Identify which harmonic terms are present, which are missing, and what the DC term is.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the mixed coefficient table. Check $a_0$ first, because it sets the DC level: $a_0=4$ means the signal has DC term $a_0/2=2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=2+0\\cos(\\omega_0 t)-3\\cos(2\\omega_0 t)+5\\sin(\\omega_0 t)+0\\sin(2\\omega_0 t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Then scan each harmonic index $k$ and test $a_k$ and $b_k$ for zero versus nonzero. At $k=1$, $a_1=0$ means no cosine term at $\\omega_0$, but $b_1=5$ means the sine term at $\\omega_0$ is present. At $k=2$, $a_2=-3$ means the cosine term at $2\\omega_0$ is present with negative weight, while $b_2=0$ means no sine term at $2\\omega_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the signal has the DC level $2$, a sine harmonic at $\\omega_0$, and a cosine harmonic at $2\\omega_0$. The zero coefficients remove only their matching terms; they do not remove the whole signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
